          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described about salt and fluid for someone living with heart failure?",
          "answer": "Here is what the retrieved material says about salt and fluid in heart failure. It is general information, not advice for your own situation.\n\n**Why salt matters.** In heart failure, sodium (the salt part) holds water in the body. A weakened heart may not move that extra fluid along, so it can back up into the lungs and legs. That is why a single high-salt meal can cause breathlessness or swelling within a day or two. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure: sodium, volume, and the daily weight*.)\n\n**Most salt is hidden.** Most dietary sodium comes from packaged and restaurant food, not from the salt shaker. (Source: same document, which refers to the *Diet for high blood pressure* note.)\n\n**Daily weight.** Rapid weight gain over a few days can signal fluid building up. Many people are given an action plan that says what weight change should prompt a call to their care team. If you have one, it takes priority over general guidance. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure*.)\n\n**Salt substitutes.** Substitutes containing potassium chloride are a decision for your own clinician, because potassium interacts with some heart failure medicines. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure*.)\n\n**Individual advice.** Sodium and fluid advice is individualized rather than one-size-fits-all. (Source: *Heart failure*, condition summary.)\n\n**Not covered.** The retrieved material did not give specific sodium or fluid amounts, so I cannot tell you a daily limit. Ask your clinician or heart failure team for your personal targets.\n\n**Get urgent help if** you have breathlessness at rest or that wakes you at night and does not ease when sitting up, chest pain, fainting, confusion, or rapid weight gain over a few days. Pink frothy sputum with severe breathlessness is an emergency: call emergency services now. (Source: *Heart failure*, \"When urgent assessment is advised.\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
